'''Mankiyali''' is an endangered [[Indo-Aryan language]] of the [[Dardic languages|Dardic]]<ref>{{cite web | url=https://rashidgabbaro.com/history-1/20200110_231244-3/ | title=Dardic languages – RASHID AHMED GABARO }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.proquest.com/openview/5eedff65f4b0b95c2a3059880e3cd428/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=105703 | title=A First Look at Mankiyali Language: An Endangered Language | website=[[ProQuest]] }}</ref> sub-branch spoken in [[Khyber Pakhtunkhwa]], northern Pakistan. It is spoken by a small community sometimes referred to as the Trawara<ref>Pronounced {{IPA|[t̪rɑʋɽɑ]}}.</ref> in the secluded village of [[Danna, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa|Danna]] (or Dana)<!--Dana: according to Munshi 2017--> located in the [[Bandi Shungli]] union council of [[Mansehra District]]<!--46{{nbsp}}km northwest of [[Mansehra]]-->.{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|p=177}} Some speakers of the language are also scattered in the neighbouring villages of [[Damaka]], [[Guldar, Pakistan|Guldar]], [[Arghaniya]], [[Chamrasi]] and [[Shoshni]].{{sfn|Anjum|2016|pp=2–3}} The whole community has been reported to be bilingual in [[Northern Hindko|Hindko]], the [[language of wider communication]] in [[Hazara region|the Hazara area]].{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|p=177}} The language is still being passed on to the next generation, but overall it is [[language shift|losing ground]] to Hindko.{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|pp=177, 179}} The first study documenting the existence of the language was published only in 2015. The name ''Mankiyali'' is of recent origin, having been coined by members of the community to replace the older terms ''Tarawara'' and ''Tarawari'', which are now seen as stigmatising.{{sfn|Munshi|2017}}

Mankiyali is not [[mutually intelligible]] with any of the surrounding languages. Lexical similarity tests have revealed that it shares a little over a third of its core vocabulary with the local varieties of [[Northern Hindko|Hindko]] and [[Gojri]], and a slightly higher percentage (41–42%) with [[Gowro language|Gowro]] and [[Bateri language|Bateri]] – two languages of the Kohistani group of [[Dardic languages|Dardic]].<ref>{{harvnb|Anjum|Rehman|2015|pp=179–81}}. The lexical similarity percentages are as follows: 34% with [[Ushojo]], 41% with Gowro, 42% with Bateri, 39% with Hazara Gojri and 36% with Sherpur Hindko.</ref> The somewhat higher similarity with Bateri could indicate a common origin, which would be consistent with the oral traditions of the community.{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|p=181}}

Mankiyali has been influenced by Hindko and Gojri, particularly in the development of [[Tone (linguistics)|phonemic tone]]: a preliminary analysis indicated that its tonal system is of the Punjabi type, shared with Hindko and Gojri and contrasting with the systems found in northern Dardic languages.{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|pp=181, 185}}<!--read Baart to make sure this sentence makes sense--> The [[voiced aspirate]]s have been lost, conditioning a low rising tone on the following vowel.{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|pp=182, 185}} There are contrasting dental ({{IPA|/ts/}}) and palatal ({{IPA|/tʃ/ /tʃʰ/}}) [[affricates]].{{sfn|Anjum|Rehman|2015|pp=182–83}}

